{"content":[{"c":["LEVI, Tribe of"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Israelite tribe taking its name from the third son of Leah and Jacob (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.29.34","r":"Gen.29.34","rawR":"Gen.29.34","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.29.34","value":"Gen.29.34"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 29:34"},"). The meaning of the name (“attached”) is a pun on Leah’s position as an unloved wife: now that she has borne three sons to Jacob, surely he will be “attached” to her. Elsewhere, by a similar pun, the tribe of Levi is described as “attached” to Aaron (",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.2","r":"Num.18.2","rawR":"Num.18.2","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.2","value":"Num.18.2"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 18:2"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"},{"c":["Levi appears with Simeon at the treacherous slaughter of the inhabitants of Canaanite Shechem (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.34.25-29","r":"Gen.34.25-29","rawR":"Gen.34.25-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.34.25-29","value":"Gen.34.25-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Gn 34:25-29"},"). This brings Jacob’s rebuke at the time (v ",{"href":"?bref=Gen.34.30","r":"Gen.34.30","rawR":"Gen.34.30","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.34.30","value":"Gen.34.30"}],"t":"ref","v":"30"},") and his deathbed curse (",{"href":"?bref=Gen.49.5-7","r":"Gen.49.5-7","rawR":"Gen.49.5-7","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Gen.49.5-7","value":"Gen.49.5-7"}],"t":"ref","v":"49:5-7"},"), foretelling that the descendants of Levi and Simeon will be scattered throughout Israel. This does not seem a likely background for God’s priestly tribe, but so it proved to be, for it was as a priestly tribe that Levi was scattered through Israel, while Simeon merely melted into the desert south of Judah."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["At first Levi was apparently a “secular” tribe like any other. True, Moses and Aaron were of Levi (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.2.1","r":"Exod.2.1","rawR":"Exod.2.1","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.2.1","value":"Exod.2.1"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ex 2:1"},"), but no stress is laid on this. Levi’s later position was God’s reward for its costly faithfulness when Israel rebelled against God (",{"href":"?bref=Exod.32.25-29","r":"Exod.32.25-29","rawR":"Exod.32.25-29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Exod.32.25-29","value":"Exod.32.25-29"}],"t":"ref","v":"32:25-29"},"); this inaugurated the “covenant with Levi” (",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.19","r":"Num.18.19","rawR":"Num.18.19","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.19","value":"Num.18.19"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 18:19"},"). Henceforth the tribe of Levi would be accepted by God instead of Israel’s firstborn sons, who belonged to him by the law of “firstfruits” (",{"href":"?bref=Num.3.11-13","r":"Num.3.11-13","rawR":"Num.3.11-13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.3.11-13","value":"Num.3.11-13"}],"t":"ref","v":"3:11-13"},"). Levi, as a tribe, could therefore own no tribal territory: God himself was their inheritance (",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.20","r":"Num.18.20","rawR":"Num.18.20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.20","value":"Num.18.20"}],"t":"ref","v":"18:20"},"). However, they were given 48 villages, with their pasturelands, in which to live (",{"href":"?bref=Josh.21.1-42","r":"Josh.21.1-42","rawR":"Josh.21.1-42","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.21.1-42","value":"Josh.21.1-42"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jos 21:1-42"},"). These included the six cities of refuge (ch ",{"href":"?bref=Josh.20.1-9","r":"Josh.20.1-9","rawR":"Josh.20.1-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Josh.20.1-9","value":"Josh.20.1-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"20"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Since Levi could not amass wealth, the tribe was to be supported by gifts and tithes (",{"href":"?bref=Num.18.21","r":"Num.18.21","rawR":"Num.18.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.18.21","value":"Num.18.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 18:21"},"); like the widow, orphan, and stranger, they were commended to the care of God’s people (",{"href":"?bref=Deut.14.29","r":"Deut.14.29","rawR":"Deut.14.29","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.14.29","value":"Deut.14.29"}],"t":"ref","v":"Dt 14:29"},"). Since they were God’s tribe, Joab was unwilling to include Levi in David’s census (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.21.6","r":"1Chr.21.6","rawR":"1Chr.21.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.21.6","value":"1Chr.21.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 21:6"},"; cf. ",{"href":"?bref=Num.1.49","r":"Num.1.49","rawR":"Num.1.49","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.1.49","value":"Num.1.49"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 1:49"},"). Naturally, Levi did not serve in war except in a religious capacity (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.20.21","r":"2Chr.20.21","rawR":"2Chr.20.21","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.20.21","value":"2Chr.20.21"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 20:21"},"). Their service pertained to the meeting tent (",{"href":"?bref=Num.1.50-53","r":"Num.1.50-53","rawR":"Num.1.50-53","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.1.50-53","value":"Num.1.50-53"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 1:50-53"},") and later the temple (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.23.25-32","r":"1Chr.23.25-32","rawR":"1Chr.23.25-32","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.23.25-32","value":"1Chr.23.25-32"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 23:25-32"},"). Within Levi the Bible makes a clear distinction between (1) the high priest (sometimes merely called “the priest,” ",{"href":"?bref=1Sam.1.9","r":"1Sam.1.9","rawR":"1Sam.1.9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Sam.1.9","value":"1Sam.1.9"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Sm 1:9"},"), who came from one branch of Aaron’s family; (2) the rest of the priests, also of particular families; and (3) a mass of subordinate Levites, who had lesser tasks. In early days they packed and moved the portable tent of meeting (",{"href":"?bref=Num.1.50-51","r":"Num.1.50-51","rawR":"Num.1.50-51","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Num.1.50-51","value":"Num.1.50-51"}],"t":"ref","v":"Nm 1:50-51"},"), as well as did other duties; in later days they served as porters and choristers (",{"href":"?bref=1Chr.16.42","r":"1Chr.16.42","rawR":"1Chr.16.42","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"1Chr.16.42","value":"1Chr.16.42"}],"t":"ref","v":"1 Chr 16:42"},"). The duties of Levi are summarized in ",{"href":"?bref=Deut.33.8-11","r":"Deut.33.8-11","rawR":"Deut.33.8-11","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Deut.33.8-11","value":"Deut.33.8-11"}],"t":"ref","v":"Deuteronomy 33:8-11"},", where oracular guidance and theological instruction are just as important as their priestly duties. It is therefore no surprise that Jehoshaphat later used them as teachers of law (",{"href":"?bref=2Chr.17.7-9","r":"2Chr.17.7-9","rawR":"2Chr.17.7-9","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Chr.17.7-9","value":"2Chr.17.7-9"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Chr 17:7-9"},"). Nevertheless, the ordinary Israelite thought of them primarily as priestly (",{"href":"?bref=Judg.17.13","r":"Judg.17.13","rawR":"Judg.17.13","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Judg.17.13","value":"Judg.17.13"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jgs 17:13"},")."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":["Later references to the lasting covenant with Levi are found in ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.33.20-26","r":"Jer.33.20-26","rawR":"Jer.33.20-26","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.33.20-26","value":"Jer.33.20-26"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jeremiah 33:20-26"}," and ",{"href":"?bref=Mal.3.3-4","r":"Mal.3.3-4","rawR":"Mal.3.3-4","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Mal.3.3-4","value":"Mal.3.3-4"}],"t":"ref","v":"Malachi 3:3-4"},". Members of the tribe returned from the exile (",{"href":"?bref=Ezra.2.36-42","r":"Ezra.2.36-42","rawR":"Ezra.2.36-42","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Ezra.2.36-42","value":"Ezra.2.36-42"}],"t":"ref","v":"Ezr 2:36-42"},"), apparently more coming from the priestly than the wider Levitical section. Barnabas, in NT days, belonged to the tribe of Levi (",{"href":"?bref=Acts.4.36","r":"Acts.4.36","rawR":"Acts.4.36","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Acts.4.36","value":"Acts.4.36"}],"t":"ref","v":"Acts 4:36"},"). Indeed, among modern Jews, wherever the surname Levy is found, a member of the tribe probably lives on."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"},{"c":[{"c":["See also"],"sourceClass":"ital","t":"span"}," ",{"href":"?item=PriestsandLevites_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","id":"PriestsandLevites_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","rawId":"PriestsandLevites_Article_TyndaleOpenBibleDictionary","t":"link","v":"Priests and Levites"},"."],"sourceClass":"","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"LeviTribeof","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"Gn 29:34","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.29.34","rawQueryValue":"Gen.29.34","sourceRefExact":"Gen.29.34"},{"anchorText":"Nm 18:2","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Num.18.2","rawQueryValue":"Num.18.2","sourceRefExact":"Num.18.2"},{"anchorText":"Gn 34:25-29","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.34.25-29","rawQueryValue":"Gen.34.25-29","sourceRefExact":"Gen.34.25-29"},{"anchorText":"30","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.34.30","rawQueryValue":"Gen.34.30","sourceRefExact":"Gen.34.30"},{"anchorText":"49:5-7","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Gen.49.5-7","rawQueryValue":"Gen.49.5-7","sourceRefExact":"Gen.49.5-7"},{"anchorText":"Ex 2:1","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Exod.2.1","rawQueryValue":"Exod.2.1","sourceRefExact":"Exod.2.1"},{"anchorText":"32:25-29","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Exod.32.25-29","rawQueryValue":"Exod.32.25-29","sourceRefExact":"Exod.32.25-29"},{"anchorText":"Nm 18:19","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Num.18.19","rawQueryValue":"Num.18.19","sourceRefExact":"Num.18.19"},{"anchorText":"3:11-13","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Num.3.11-13","rawQueryValue":"Num.3.11-13","sourceRefExact":"Num.3.11-13"},{"anchorText":"18:20","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hrefExact":"?bref=Num.18.20","rawQueryValue":"Num.18.20","sourceRefExact":"Num.18.20"},{"anchorText":"Jos 21:1-42","canonicalization":"deferred_to_dictionary_mapping_workstream","hre